Requested Badges

We request the following two artifact badges.

1. Available

We provide all necessary research artifacts through permanent public repositories.

The source code and benchmark dataset are archived on Zenodo (DOI: 10.5281/zenodo.21063932), ensuring permanent public availability.
Due to Zenodo's storage limitations, the Docker images for the target web agents are hosted on Docker Hub.
If the Docker Hub images become unavailable in the future, the target agents can also be obtained from their respective official repositories, which are referenced in our paper.

Together, these resources provide long-term public access to all components required to reproduce our experiments.

2. Reusable

We believe our artifact satisfies the requirements for the Reusable badge for the following reasons.

First, AgentBreaker is designed with a modular architecture that cleanly separates the four core components — Composer, LM Tester, Evaluator, and Deployer — each implemented as an independent module under framework/. 
Researchers can replace or extend any individual module without modifying the rest of the pipeline.

Second, the artifact is straightforwardly extensible to new target agents. 
Each agent integration consists of a self-contained directory under agents/{agent_name}/, which includes agent-specific edit files and a Docker Compose configuration generated by generate_agents_compose_file.py. 
Adding support for a new web agent requires only providing the corresponding edit files and a Docker image, with no changes to the core AgentBreaker framework.

Third, the benchmark dataset (60 HTML pages from Online-Mind2Web) and the attack targets (TERMINATE, CLICK, INPUT, PROMPT) are fully configurable via a single .env file, allowing researchers to evaluate new agents or action types with minimal effort.

Finally, the artifact includes a smoke test (test_deploy_execute.py) and comprehensive documentation covering installation, configuration, and step-by-step reproduction of all results in Table 1 and Table 3 of the paper.

Together, these design choices make AgentBreaker a practical foundation for future research on indirect prompt injection attacks against web agents.
